WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.501
An American business mogul

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.570
is getting rid of his private jet

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:06.106
because he's worried about the climate.

00:00:06.106 --> 00:00:06.706
Stephen Prince,

00:00:06.706 --> 00:00:07.941
the co founder, the founder

00:00:07.941 --> 00:00:09.609
and owner of a gift card business,

00:00:09.609 --> 00:00:11.911
is selling a Cessna Citation three.

00:00:11.911 --> 00:00:14.047
It should raise over a million or so.

00:00:14.047 --> 00:00:15.315
He says he realized

00:00:15.315 --> 00:00:17.317
his private jet use was a travesty

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:18.785
for the environment.

00:00:18.785 --> 00:00:20.153
And the numbers back it up.

00:00:20.153 --> 00:00:21.321
Compared to commercial travel,

00:00:21.321 --> 00:00:22.922
getting around by private jet emits

00:00:22.922 --> 00:00:25.592
at least ten times more pollution

00:00:25.825 --> 00:00:26.960
per passenger.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:27.160
Mr.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.361
Prince is also the vice chair

00:00:28.361 --> 00:00:29.662
of the Patriotic Millionaires

00:00:29.662 --> 00:00:31.598
Group, a group of wealthy Americans

00:00:31.598 --> 00:00:33.400
for higher taxes for rich people.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.301
He's with me now. Good to see you, sir.

00:00:35.301 --> 00:00:36.302
Thank you.

00:00:36.436 --> 00:00:38.605
I admire your honesty

00:00:38.605 --> 00:00:40.140
in a sense of admitting

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:41.941
you love the private jet.

00:00:41.941 --> 00:00:42.575
One becomes

00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:44.611
addicted to its convenience,

00:00:44.611 --> 00:00:47.280
but you are still prepared to give it up.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:48.481
What was the

00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:51.351
what was the the Damascus moment for you?

00:00:53.019 --> 00:00:53.386
A New

00:00:53.386 --> 00:00:54.220
York Times ran

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:57.257
an article back in the late winter, early

00:00:57.257 --> 00:00:58.925
spring that they outlined

00:00:58.925 --> 00:00:59.793
some of the numbers

00:00:59.793 --> 00:01:01.528
that you just mentioned about the

00:01:01.528 --> 00:01:04.330
the horrible abuse that the private jet

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:06.666
travel creates in terms of

00:01:06.666 --> 00:01:09.402
carbon input into the environment and

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:11.538
I just feel like I have an obligation to

00:01:11.538 --> 00:01:13.673
to protect the environment

00:01:13.673 --> 00:01:15.742
in general and future generations.

00:01:15.742 --> 00:01:16.276
And the

00:01:16.276 --> 00:01:18.344
and the travesty that is being called us

00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:21.614
that's caused by some of our abuse.

00:01:21.614 --> 00:01:23.149
The you you

00:01:23.149 --> 00:01:25.085
you are going to continue

00:01:25.085 --> 00:01:26.553
releasing a smaller,

00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:28.421
more fuel efficient plane

00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:30.323
and but you're not necessarily

00:01:30.323 --> 00:01:31.024
going to

00:01:31.024 --> 00:01:32.992
encourage or proselytize others

00:01:32.992 --> 00:01:34.360
to do the same.

00:01:34.994 --> 00:01:36.663
Absolutely not everyone.

00:01:36.663 --> 00:01:37.597
I wish that

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:38.031
I thought

00:01:38.031 --> 00:01:39.532
that would be effective to do that.

00:01:39.532 --> 00:01:41.234
But it's as I said

00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:42.902
in the article that you're talking about,

00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:43.803
it is addictive.

00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:46.005
It's hard. It's a hard thing to give up.

00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:47.607
Flying privately is probably

00:01:47.607 --> 00:01:49.242
the coolest thing that I've ever done

00:01:49.242 --> 00:01:51.744
that money has provided me the access to.

00:01:51.744 --> 00:01:53.613
But to delude myself

00:01:53.613 --> 00:01:54.347
into believing that I'm

00:01:54.347 --> 00:01:55.081
going to convince my

00:01:55.081 --> 00:01:56.216
other wealthy friends to do

00:01:56.216 --> 00:01:58.251
the same thing is is pretty naive.

00:01:58.251 --> 00:01:59.452
That's just not going to happen.

00:01:59.452 --> 00:02:01.321
But I feel I've got to put my money

00:02:01.321 --> 00:02:02.522
where my mouth is in terms

00:02:02.522 --> 00:02:03.790
of this climate issue.

00:02:04.991 --> 00:02:07.227
What about fractional ownership?

00:02:07.227 --> 00:02:08.761
The NetJets option now?

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:09.062
You know,

00:02:09.062 --> 00:02:11.131
I suppose one could arguably say,

00:02:11.498 --> 00:02:12.432
and I realize I'm

00:02:12.432 --> 00:02:14.567
stretching the point, Stephen, I read.

00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:15.668
But, you know,

00:02:15.668 --> 00:02:18.004
in the case of sort of common ownership,

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:19.139
if you're like

00:02:20.039 --> 00:02:22.075
NetJets is the closest you come

00:02:22.275 --> 00:02:25.612
to the bifurcation of private jets.

00:02:25.612 --> 00:02:25.912
At least

00:02:25.912 --> 00:02:29.449
many people are using the same planes

00:02:29.449 --> 00:02:31.551
Well, Richard, look, it's just it's

00:02:31.551 --> 00:02:32.218
just another bite

00:02:32.218 --> 00:02:33.720
of the same desert, right?

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:34.954
Instead of having the whole

00:02:34.954 --> 00:02:36.189
the whole slice of pie,

00:02:36.189 --> 00:02:37.423
I'm just taking a bite over

00:02:37.423 --> 00:02:38.458
when I do that.

00:02:38.458 --> 00:02:39.993
The numbers are still the same.

00:02:39.993 --> 00:02:42.028
If you get on a NetJets flight and you

00:02:42.028 --> 00:02:42.328
you know,

00:02:42.328 --> 00:02:45.498
you reduce the damage to some extent

00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:47.267
when you get on with four people.

00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:48.635
So so you're you know,

00:02:48.635 --> 00:02:49.836
you're not doing

00:02:49.836 --> 00:02:51.771
as much harm individually,

00:02:51.771 --> 00:02:52.539
but it's still it's

00:02:52.539 --> 00:02:54.240
just an incredibly selfish thing to do.

00:02:55.341 --> 00:02:56.876
It's I'm not

00:02:56.876 --> 00:02:57.177
I'm not

00:02:57.177 --> 00:02:58.378
going to change the world

00:02:58.378 --> 00:03:00.914
at 22,000 private jets on the planet.

00:03:00.914 --> 00:03:03.149
16,000 of which are in the United States.

00:03:03.449 --> 00:03:05.585
And we're the worst abusers of it.

00:03:05.585 --> 00:03:06.452
And the other side of it

00:03:06.452 --> 00:03:07.720
is we're not paying our fair share.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:08.221
I mean,

00:03:08.221 --> 00:03:09.589
you know, our fuel prices,

00:03:09.589 --> 00:03:12.492
we're using a fairly significant

00:03:12.492 --> 00:03:13.960
part of the FAA

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:15.628
resources in the United States.

00:03:15.628 --> 00:03:17.130
But being a fraction of that

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:19.232
and in terms of the revenue

00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:20.433
that we're paying into the system.

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:22.902
So we're getting a really sweet deal

00:03:22.902 --> 00:03:25.038
and we don't want to pay homage to that.

00:03:25.672 --> 00:03:27.040
Now, this is very interesting.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:27.774
Your your work

00:03:27.774 --> 00:03:29.342
with project millionaires,

00:03:29.342 --> 00:03:33.313
because the argument is, you know,

00:03:33.546 --> 00:03:34.414
say increase

00:03:34.414 --> 00:03:38.251
the sales tax increase the fuel tax.

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:39.419
In other words,

00:03:39.419 --> 00:03:42.889
make a higher percentage payment

00:03:43.256 --> 00:03:45.558
for the abuse of the infrastructure

00:03:45.558 --> 00:03:46.826
in the facilities.

00:03:46.826 --> 00:03:49.796
To that extent, that's an economic way

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:51.564
of redressing the balance

00:03:53.533 --> 00:03:54.400
Well, given

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:55.401
the fact that,

00:03:55.401 --> 00:03:56.269
as I said earlier,

00:03:56.269 --> 00:03:57.036
I don't think I'm going to get

00:03:57.036 --> 00:03:58.204
my wealthy friends to do it.

00:03:58.204 --> 00:03:59.505
And just really

00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:02.175
it's kind of amusing to me when when

00:04:02.175 --> 00:04:03.109
for whatever reason

00:04:03.109 --> 00:04:05.211
something happens in the Middle East or,

00:04:05.211 --> 00:04:06.346
or even in the US

00:04:06.346 --> 00:04:07.647
and the price of fuel

00:04:07.647 --> 00:04:08.948
doubles, as it did

00:04:08.948 --> 00:04:10.116
earlier last year,

00:04:10.116 --> 00:04:12.485
fuel went from $3 a gallon glass

00:04:12.785 --> 00:04:13.453
you know,

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:16.623
a fuel for jet went from $3 a gallon

00:04:16.623 --> 00:04:20.426
to $5.50 and $6 a gallon in some places.

00:04:20.627 --> 00:04:21.194
And all

00:04:21.194 --> 00:04:23.396
everyone did was grumble a little bit

00:04:23.396 --> 00:04:25.398
about Shell and Exxon and,

00:04:25.598 --> 00:04:26.332
and BP,

00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:28.067
and everybody is making a lot of money.

00:04:28.067 --> 00:04:29.068
But if instead

00:04:29.068 --> 00:04:30.570
we want to double the taxes,

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:32.105
which is $0.21

00:04:32.105 --> 00:04:35.842
a gallon right now to $0.42 $0.21

00:04:35.842 --> 00:04:36.209
and we're

00:04:36.209 --> 00:04:38.344
and we will go crazy

00:04:38.344 --> 00:04:39.112
when that happens

00:04:39.112 --> 00:04:40.546
because we're giving to the government

00:04:40.546 --> 00:04:41.948
in the form of taxes.

00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:43.950
It to me it's a double standard.

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:45.051
We let all Exxon

00:04:45.051 --> 00:04:46.052
and all the oil companies

00:04:46.052 --> 00:04:47.387
make this incredible amount

00:04:47.387 --> 00:04:48.888
