00:05:37.837 --> 00:05:38.638
we can go back,

00:05:38.638 --> 00:05:41.307
as I said, to what we had in the 1960s,

00:05:41.674 --> 00:05:44.544
which was ultimately a 90% tax rate

00:05:44.777 --> 00:05:46.346
on on the last dollar,

00:05:46.346 --> 00:05:47.613
whether that's a 10 million

00:05:47.613 --> 00:05:49.515
or 50 million or 100 million.

00:05:49.515 --> 00:05:50.783
But what we're testing ourselves

00:05:50.783 --> 00:05:52.852
right now is non-sustainable.

00:05:52.852 --> 00:05:54.921
We cannot continue to run our government

00:05:54.921 --> 00:05:55.855
and the world

00:05:55.855 --> 00:05:57.156
and our present tax policy

00:05:57.156 --> 00:05:58.725
and nobody wants to talk about, you know,

00:05:58.725 --> 00:05:59.726
they're not even calling it

00:05:59.726 --> 00:06:00.827
taxes anymore.

00:06:00.827 --> 00:06:02.161
They're calling it revenue.

00:06:02.161 --> 00:06:03.062
And is that what it is?

00:06:03.062 --> 00:06:03.730
Of course it is.

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:04.931
But at the same time, it

00:06:04.931 --> 00:06:07.100
it has an incredibly pejorative sound.

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:09.669
When we call it taxes, it is what it is.

00:06:09.669 --> 00:06:11.337
We have to pay the price

00:06:11.337 --> 00:06:13.406
to to fund our various governments.

00:06:13.406 --> 00:06:15.541
In my case, the United States government.

00:06:15.942 --> 00:06:17.377
Stephen, we'll talk more.

00:06:17.377 --> 00:06:18.511
Love having you on the program

00:06:18.511 --> 00:06:19.145
tonight, sir.

00:06:19.145 --> 00:06:21.013
Very grateful for you. Thank you.

